From a7275281e160997a4d18cfd971af94769f245c02 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Angell Fear <angell.fear@gmail.com>
Date: Thu, 24 Sep 2026 11:20:37 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] panfrost: wait for in-flight jobs on JM context teardown
 without a kernel context

Without an explicit JM context (kernels that predate
DRM_IOCTL_PANFROST_JM_CTX_CREATE), jm_cleanup_context() returns straight
away, and panfrost_destroy() goes on to release the context while jobs it
submitted are still in flight. Those jobs reference the screen-wide shader
and descriptor pools, whose BOs are in no job's BO list, so the kernel does
not keep them alive. When an application tears down without a glFinish(),
the pools are freed under the GPU: the running job takes an MMU
translation fault, and every job still queued faults as well and hits the
500 ms scheduler timeout.

This reproduces every time with

  GST_GL_PLATFORM=egl GST_GL_WINDOW=surfaceless gst-launch-1.0 \
    gltestsrc pattern=mandelbrot num-buffers=300 ! \
    'video/x-raw(memory:GLMemory),width=1920,height=1080' ! fakesink

on a Mali-G52 (panfrost JM, no JM context support in the kernel): 40-60
"Unhandled Page fault" / "gpu sched timeout" pairs and ~24 s to exit.
PAN_MESA_DEBUG=sync, or a glFinish() before exit, makes it go away.

With an explicit JM context, DRM_IOCTL_PANFROST_JM_CTX_DESTROY kills any
in-flight job, so only the legacy path needs this: wait there on the
context syncobj, the out-fence of the last job submitted.

Reviewed-by: Boris Brezillon <boris.brezillon@collabora.com>
---
 src/gallium/drivers/panfrost/pan_jm.c | 16 ++++++++++++++--
 1 file changed, 14 insertions(+), 2 deletions(-)

diff --git a/src/gallium/drivers/panfrost/pan_jm.c b/src/gallium/drivers/panfrost/pan_jm.c
index d14fc05082fb..18dcdecb96e0 100644
--- a/src/gallium/drivers/panfrost/pan_jm.c
+++ b/src/gallium/drivers/panfrost/pan_jm.c
@@ -1138,10 +1138,22 @@ GENX(jm_cleanup_context)(struct panfrost_context *ctx)
 {
    PAN_TRACE_FUNC(PAN_TRACE_GL_JM);
 
-   if (!ctx->jm.handle)
+   struct panfrost_device *dev = pan_device(ctx->base.screen);
+
+   if (!ctx->jm.handle) {
+      /* Without an explicit JM context, nothing kills the jobs this context
+       * still has in flight, and they point at memory that is in no job's BO
+       * list -- the screen-wide shader and descriptor pools -- so nothing in
+       * the kernel keeps it alive either. An application that tears down
+       * without a glFinish() would have it released under the GPU, and every
+       * job still queued would fault and hit the scheduler timeout. The
+       * context syncobj is the out-fence of the last job submitted.
+       */
+      drmSyncobjWait(panfrost_device_fd(dev), &ctx->syncobj, 1, INT64_MAX, 0,
+                     NULL);
       return;
+   }
 
-   struct panfrost_device *dev = pan_device(ctx->base.screen);
    struct drm_panfrost_jm_ctx_destroy args = {
       .handle = ctx->jm.handle,
    };
